1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,963
Here's a failure pattern you will see, almost guaranteed, on any feature that

2
00:00:04,963 --> 00:00:07,478
summarises or reasons over a conversation.

3
00:00:07,478 --> 00:00:11,672
It works perfectly in the demo, on a short thread you picked yourself.

4
00:00:11,672 --> 00:00:16,464
Weeks later, on a real forty-message support thread, it starts missing things it

5
00:00:16,464 --> 00:00:17,302
used to catch.

6
00:00:17,302 --> 00:00:18,860
Nobody changed the prompt.

7
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:20,358
Nobody changed the model.

8
00:00:20,358 --> 00:00:24,970
What changed is length, and length runs straight into a hard limit called the

9
00:00:24,970 --> 00:00:30,002
context window: the finite amount of text a model can hold in view at once, measured

10
00:00:30,002 --> 00:00:31,619
not in words but in tokens.

11
00:00:31,619 --> 00:00:36,471
Understanding that limit, and what happens right at it, is the difference between

12
00:00:36,471 --> 00:00:40,724
debugging this in an afternoon and debugging it by guessing for a week.

13
00:00:40,724 --> 00:00:43,360
And it will look like a model problem first.

14
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:48,092
Someone will suggest switching providers, or rewriting the prompt for the fifth

15
00:00:48,092 --> 00:00:48,392
time.

16
00:00:48,392 --> 00:00:50,429
The actual fix is usually neither.

17
00:00:50,979 --> 00:00:55,752
First, the unit these models actually think in, because "word count" will mislead

18
00:00:55,752 --> 00:00:55,988
you.

19
00:00:55,988 --> 00:01:00,585
A token is roughly three quarters of a word: short common words are one token,

20
00:01:00,585 --> 00:01:03,237
longer or rarer ones split into two or three.

21
00:01:03,237 --> 00:01:08,011
If you want the exact splitting rules, they don't change any decision you own, so

22
00:01:08,011 --> 00:01:12,196
the number to remember is the rough ratio, not the mechanics behind it.

23
00:01:12,196 --> 00:01:16,910
What matters is the practical size: a typical support ticket reply runs forty to

24
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:17,677
sixty tokens.

25
00:01:17,677 --> 00:01:22,274
That number matters in a minute, because your context window has a fixed token

26
00:01:22,274 --> 00:01:27,047
budget, and every part of your prompt, the system instructions, the conversation,

27
00:01:27,047 --> 00:01:30,525
the documents you retrieved, is drawing from the same pool.

28
00:01:31,075 --> 00:01:34,866
Picture that ticket from Section 0, still open, still escalating.

29
00:01:34,866 --> 00:01:39,706
By message forty-five, the thread is long enough that it no longer fits the context

30
00:01:39,706 --> 00:01:40,464
window whole.

31
00:01:40,464 --> 00:01:45,247
The common, simple fix engineering reaches for first is a sliding window: keep the

32
00:01:45,247 --> 00:01:48,571
most recent messages, drop the oldest ones until it fits.

33
00:01:48,571 --> 00:01:53,178
Straightforward, cheap, and it means the first message, the one with the actual

34
00:01:53,178 --> 00:01:56,969
deadline, is exactly the one most likely to get silently dropped.

35
00:01:56,969 --> 00:01:59,069
Nobody decided to lose the deadline.

36
00:01:59,069 --> 00:02:03,443
The window filled up, and the oldest content, which happened to be the most

37
00:02:03,443 --> 00:02:05,659
important content, lost the tie-break.

38
00:02:05,659 --> 00:02:08,575
This is also why it's so hard to catch in testing.

39
00:02:08,575 --> 00:02:11,433
Fifty short, tidy test cases will not surface it.

40
00:02:11,433 --> 00:02:16,215
A length-dependent failure only shows up in a test case that's actually allowed to

41
00:02:16,215 --> 00:02:20,647
grow long, which is precisely the kind of case most test sets never include.

42
00:02:21,197 --> 00:02:24,487
Here's why the window fills up faster than people expect.

43
00:02:24,487 --> 00:02:26,276
It isn't just the conversation.

44
00:02:26,276 --> 00:02:28,584
Four things share the exact same budget.

45
00:02:28,584 --> 00:02:32,278
Your system instructions take a slice, fixed, every single call.

46
00:02:32,278 --> 00:02:36,780
The conversation history takes a slice, and it's the one that grows over time.

47
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:39,954
Any documents you retrieve for the answer take a slice.

48
00:02:39,954 --> 00:02:44,628
And whatever's left over is the room the model actually has to write its response

49
00:02:44,628 --> 00:02:44,802
in.

50
00:02:44,802 --> 00:02:49,419
Grow the conversation history without bound, and you're not just risking dropped

51
00:02:49,419 --> 00:02:49,938
messages.

52
00:02:49,938 --> 00:02:54,613
You're shrinking the room left for the model to answer in, and a cramped response

53
00:02:54,613 --> 00:02:57,671
budget produces answers that get cut off mid-thought.

54
00:02:58,221 --> 00:03:03,041
Say the honest caveat, because "just use a bigger context window" is the answer

55
00:03:03,041 --> 00:03:05,360
people reach for, and it's incomplete.

56
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:10,119
Modern models do hold enormous windows now, sometimes hundreds of thousands of

57
00:03:10,119 --> 00:03:10,546
tokens.

58
00:03:10,546 --> 00:03:14,023
But fitting is not the same claim as attending to evenly.

59
00:03:14,023 --> 00:03:19,087
Research on these models consistently finds that content buried deep in a very long

60
00:03:19,087 --> 00:03:23,846
context gets less reliable recall than content near the start or the very end.

61
00:03:23,846 --> 00:03:26,165
Fitting solves the truncation problem.

62
00:03:26,165 --> 00:03:29,399
It doesn't fully solve the getting-forgotten problem.

63
00:03:29,399 --> 00:03:32,998
And every token you send costs money, on every single call.

64
00:03:32,998 --> 00:03:35,683
Section seven puts an actual number on that.

65
00:03:35,683 --> 00:03:40,747
For now, just know that "send everything, every time" is a cost decision as much as

66
00:03:40,747 --> 00:03:41,906
an engineering one.

67
00:03:42,456 --> 00:03:46,136
Name the fix precisely, because "add more context" is not it.

68
00:03:46,136 --> 00:03:49,937
Context management means deciding, deliberately, what survives.

69
00:03:49,937 --> 00:03:54,703
Summarise older messages into a few sentences instead of keeping them verbatim.

70
00:03:54,703 --> 00:03:59,710
Re-inject the details that must never be lost, like a deadline, near the end of the

71
00:03:59,710 --> 00:04:03,330
prompt where recall is strongest, not buried at message one.

72
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:08,036
Decide this on purpose, because the alternative is a sliding window making the

73
00:04:08,036 --> 00:04:11,173
decision for you, silently, the same way every time.

74
00:04:11,173 --> 00:04:16,120
The exercise is one honest question: for your own feature, what happens at message

75
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:16,482
fifty?

76
00:04:16,482 --> 00:04:20,765
If the answer is "we haven't tried it," that's not a hypothetical risk.

77
00:04:20,765 --> 00:04:24,626
That's an untested path your real users are going to find first.

78
00:04:25,176 --> 00:04:26,968
One thing before you move on.

79
00:04:26,968 --> 00:04:32,036
Find a real case for your feature: more messages, a longer document, whatever your

80
00:04:32,036 --> 00:04:34,013
feature actually takes as input.

81
00:04:34,013 --> 00:04:39,081
Push it to three times the length of the longest one you've genuinely handled, run

82
00:04:39,081 --> 00:04:43,963
it, and check whether the one fact you'd hate to lose, the equivalent of Ticket

83
00:04:43,963 --> 00:04:46,621
4471's deadline, survived to the other end.

84
00:04:46,621 --> 00:04:51,194
Next lecture stays inside this same mechanism, but from a different angle:

85
00:04:51,194 --> 00:04:56,014
temperature, and why the same prompt, on the same day, gives you two different

86
00:04:56,014 --> 00:04:56,509
answers.
